1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">In computer vision, models should adapt gracefully
when image resolution changes,</font>

2
00:00:05,727 --> 00:00:08,034
<font color="#FFFFFF">a property called scale-equivariance.</font>

3
00:00:08,114 --> 00:00:10,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, from Purdue University,</font>

4
00:00:10,372 --> 00:00:15,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">points out that existing scale-equivariant CNNs are
not truly scale-equivariant,</font>

5
00:00:15,535 --> 00:00:21,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">because they formulate down-scaling in the
continuous domain and ignore anti-aliasing.</font>

6
00:00:21,084 --> 00:00:27,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors instead formulate down-scaling directly
in the discrete domain with anti-aliasing,</font>

7
00:00:27,149 --> 00:00:33,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">and build a new family of deep nets from Fourier
layers that achieves absolute zero equivariance error,</font>

8
00:00:33,795 --> 00:00:39,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">both in theory and in practice, while staying
competitive on classification accuracy.</font>

9
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:43,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scale-equivariance means that when an object in an
image is resized,</font>

10
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:47,312
<font color="#FFFFFF">the network's features should transform consistently,</font>

11
00:00:47,392 --> 00:00:49,571
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its label should stay the same.</font>

12
00:00:49,651 --> 00:00:56,221
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recent scale-equivariant convolutional networks
pursue this through weight-sharing and kernel resizing,</font>

13
00:00:56,301 --> 00:00:59,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">using the same but resized kernel across scales.</font>

14
00:00:59,399 --> 00:01:03,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that these methods are derived in
the continuous domain,</font>

15
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:06,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">then discretized when implemented.</font>

16
00:01:06,178 --> 00:01:10,746
<font color="#FFFFFF">That discretization step introduces a
non-negligible equivariance error,</font>

17
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:15,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the networks are only approximately
scale-equivariant, not truly so.</font>

18
00:01:15,710 --> 00:01:22,165
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight is that down-scaling a discrete
signal is fundamentally a signal-processing operation.</font>

19
00:01:22,245 --> 00:01:26,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">The Nyquist sampling theorem tells us that before
subsampling,</font>

20
00:01:26,217 --> 00:01:28,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">we must apply an anti-aliasing filter,</font>

21
00:01:28,652 --> 00:01:32,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">otherwise high-frequency content folds down into
lower frequencies,</font>

22
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:36,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">the classic aliasing artifact seen in the
wagon-wheel effect.</font>

23
00:01:36,852 --> 00:01:38,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior scale-equivariant networks,</font>

24
00:01:38,967 --> 00:01:42,346
<font color="#FFFFFF">because they were formulated in the continuous domain,</font>

25
00:01:42,426 --> 00:01:44,653
<font color="#FFFFFF">simply had no place for this filter.</font>

26
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:48,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue that to be truly scale-equivariant,</font>

27
00:01:48,128 --> 00:01:52,405
<font color="#FFFFFF">you must formulate the down-scaling directly in the
discrete domain,</font>

28
00:01:52,485 --> 00:01:55,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">with anti-aliasing built in from the start.</font>

29
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:59,418
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

30
00:01:59,498 --> 00:02:03,376
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

31
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:07,335
<font color="#FFFFFF">it formulates the down-scaling operation directly
in the discrete domain as ideal downsampling,</font>

32
00:02:07,415 --> 00:02:11,293
<font color="#FFFFFF">properly accounting for anti-aliasing.</font>

33
00:02:11,373 --> 00:02:15,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it proposes a whole family of deep nets
that are truly scale-equivariant,</font>

34
00:02:15,331 --> 00:02:19,209
<font color="#FFFFFF">by rethinking every component, convolution layers,
non-linearities, and pooling,</font>

35
00:02:19,289 --> 00:02:23,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">and re-expressing them as Fourier layers that obey
a simple frequency-dependency rule.</font>

36
00:02:23,247 --> 00:02:27,125
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, through extensive experiments on MNIST-scale
and STL-10,</font>

37
00:02:27,205 --> 00:02:31,084
<font color="#FFFFFF">it shows the model attains an absolute zero
end-to-end scale-equivariance error while</font>

38
00:02:31,164 --> 00:02:35,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">remaining competitive in classification accuracy
and more data-efficient in low-resource</font>

39
00:02:35,122 --> 00:02:39,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">settings.</font>

40
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:43,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method starts from a precise definition of
down-scaling: ideal downsampling,</font>

41
00:02:43,787 --> 00:02:48,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">which first applies an ideal low-pass filter that
zeros out all frequencies above the new Nyquist limit,</font>

42
00:02:48,193 --> 00:02:52,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">then subsamples.</font>

43
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:56,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">From this the authors derive their central condition,</font>

44
00:02:57,007 --> 00:03:01,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">Claim 1: a deep net is truly scale-equivariant if
and only if every output frequency term</font>

45
00:03:01,413 --> 00:03:05,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">depends only on input frequency terms that are
equal or lower.</font>

46
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:10,147
<font color="#FFFFFF">They then redesign each network component to
satisfy this rule.</font>

47
00:03:10,227 --> 00:03:14,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">A spatially-local Fourier layer constrains the
kernel so it learns local features while staying equivariant.</font>

48
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:18,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">A scale-equivariant non-linearity applies ReLU
frequency-band by frequency-band.</font>

49
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:23,367
<font color="#FFFFFF">A Fourier pooling layer preserves the same
dependency structure.</font>

50
00:03:23,447 --> 00:03:27,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of the equivariant feature extractor,</font>

51
00:03:27,853 --> 00:03:32,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">they add a classifier that predicts once per scale,</font>

52
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:36,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">sharing a single MLP across scales through Fourier
padding,</font>

53
00:03:36,667 --> 00:03:40,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">and train it with a hinge consistency loss that
penalizes the model whenever a higher-resolution</font>

54
00:03:41,073 --> 00:03:45,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">image is predicted worse than its lower-resolution
version.</font>

55
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:49,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors follow prior work and evaluate on two
benchmarks.</font>

56
00:03:49,937 --> 00:03:55,854
<font color="#FFFFFF">MNIST-scale is built by randomly downsampling MNIST
digits so that every resolution from</font>

57
00:03:55,934 --> 00:04:00,829
<font color="#FFFFFF">eight-by-eight up to twenty-eight-by-twenty-eight
is equally represented.</font>

58
00:04:00,909 --> 00:04:05,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">STL10-scale applies the same construction to
natural color images,</font>

59
00:04:05,407 --> 00:04:09,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning resolutions from forty-eight up to ninety-seven.</font>

60
00:04:09,291 --> 00:04:12,482
<font color="#FFFFFF">They study performance under ideal downsampling,</font>

61
00:04:12,562 --> 00:04:17,321
<font color="#FFFFFF">where theory exactly matches practice,
generalization to unseen scales,</font>

62
00:04:17,401 --> 00:04:20,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">data efficiency at 5k, 2.5k, and 1k training samples,</font>

63
00:04:21,013 --> 00:04:26,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a harder non-ideal downsampling setting where
the anti-aliasing filter is imperfect.</font>

64
00:04:27,310 --> 00:04:28,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are striking.</font>

65
00:04:28,904 --> 00:04:31,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MNIST-scale with ideal downsampling,</font>

66
00:04:31,390 --> 00:04:36,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model achieves the highest accuracy at
ninety-eight point nine percent,</font>

67
00:04:36,172 --> 00:04:40,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">the highest scale-consistency at ninety-seven
percent, and, crucially,</font>

68
00:04:40,635 --> 00:04:42,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">an absolute zero equivariance error,</font>

69
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:47,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">while competing methods like DISCO show errors
around zero point four.</font>

70
00:04:47,393 --> 00:04:52,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">The advantage is largest on the harder STL10-scale
natural-image benchmark,</font>

71
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:59,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the model reaches seventy-three percent
accuracy against roughly fifty-eight percent for the strongest baseline,</font>

72
00:04:59,698 --> 00:05:04,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">a gain of about fifteen points, still with exactly
zero equivariance error.</font>

73
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:10,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">It also degrades gracefully: even under non-ideal
downsampling it remains the best model,</font>

74
00:05:10,154 --> 00:05:14,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in low-data regimes it is the most
data-efficient of all methods tested.</font>

75
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:19,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation isolates the consistency loss.</font>

76
00:05:19,088 --> 00:05:22,797
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across training-set sizes of five thousand,
twenty-five hundred,</font>

77
00:05:22,877 --> 00:05:26,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">and one thousand samples,</font>

78
00:05:26,665 --> 00:05:30,373
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding the consistency loss consistently improves
both accuracy and the scale-consistency rate.</font>

79
00:05:30,453 --> 00:05:34,162
<font color="#FFFFFF">For example,</font>

80
00:05:34,242 --> 00:05:37,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">at five thousand samples the scale-consistency
rises from ninety-one and a half percent</font>

81
00:05:38,030 --> 00:05:41,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">to nearly ninety-three percent.</font>

82
00:05:41,818 --> 00:05:45,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">This confirms that the hinge consistency loss is
doing real work,</font>

83
00:05:45,607 --> 00:05:49,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">encouraging the model to make better predictions as
resolution increases.</font>

84
00:05:49,395 --> 00:05:53,103
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also note that applying the
non-linearity directly in the frequency domain,</font>

85
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:56,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">though equivariant, hurt classification,</font>

86
00:05:56,972 --> 00:06:00,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is why they designed the spatial-domain
scale-equivariant non-linearity.</font>

87
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:07,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting takeaway is that scale-equivariance
should be treated as a signal-processing problem.</font>

88
00:06:07,243 --> 00:06:09,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">Once you formulate down-scaling as ideal,</font>

89
00:06:09,857 --> 00:06:16,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">anti-aliased downsampling and require every output
frequency to depend only on equal or lower input frequencies,</font>

90
00:06:16,996 --> 00:06:21,889
<font color="#FFFFFF">you can build networks from Fourier layers that are
exactly scale-equivariant,</font>

91
00:06:21,969 --> 00:06:27,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">with provably zero error rather than the small
residual errors that plagued earlier methods.</font>

92
00:06:27,833 --> 00:06:33,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">And this theoretical guarantee comes at no
practical cost: the model matches or beats</font>

93
00:06:33,252 --> 00:06:37,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">prior scale-equivariant CNNs on accuracy and is
more data-efficient,</font>

94
00:06:37,586 --> 00:06:40,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially on challenging natural images.</font>
